---
// Why build it: the first site's About, as one page the knob scrolls.
import { repositories, site } from "../data/site";
import Deck from "../layouts/Deck.astro";
import { photo } from "../lib/photos";

const credits = [
  {
    name: "Mixxx",
    href: site.mixxx,
    what: "The free, open-source DJ software the deck runs. TriMixxx is a fork of Mixxx 2.5.6; everything that makes a deck play music starts there.",
  },
  {
    name: "ttymidi",
    href: "https://github.com/usr-ein/ttymidi",
    what: "varal's serial-to-ALSA MIDI bridge, forked to carry SysEx both ways: the only thing between the controller's UART and Mixxx.",
  },
  {
    name: "ch32fun",
    href: "https://github.com/cnlohr/ch32fun",
    what: "cnlohr's bare-metal toolkit for the CH32V003, and its minichlink flasher. Every pad node runs on it.",
  },
  {
    name: "cdj2000-emulator",
    href: "https://github.com/cdj2k-revival/cdj2000-emulator",
    what: "cdj2k-revival's emulator of the CDJ-2000, which boots the player's own firmware. TriMixxx's fork puts emulated CDJ-2000NXS players on the decks' network.",
  },
  {
    name: "RAUC",
    href: "https://rauc.io",
    what: "The update framework behind the deck's A/B slots and its signed bundles.",
  },
  {
    name: "Chocolate Doom",
    href: "https://www.chocolate-doom.org",
    what: "The faithful DOOM port the deck boots into when PLAY is held, unmodified.",
  },
  {
    name: "Raspberry Pi OS, KiCad, PlatformIO",
    href: "https://www.kicad.org",
    what: "The system the deck boots, the tool every board was drawn in, and the build system of its controller firmware.",
  },
  {
    name: "The Pro DJ Link community",
    href: "https://github.com/Deep-Symmetry/dysentery",
    what: "Deep Symmetry's dysentery and the other published Pro DJ Link projects were the starting map. TriMixxx's own implementation was then written from captures of its own hardware, without their code.",
  },
] as const;

const share = await photo("2026-10-02-img-9266");
---

<Deck
  title="Why build it"
  description="Why build a DJ deck at all: who makes TriMixxx, what it stands on, its repositories and licences."
  image={share?.data.image}
  imageAlt={share?.data.alt}
  kind="page"
  back="/browse/"
  crumbs={[{ label: "Why build it", href: "/why/" }]}
>
  <article class="page">
    <h1>Why build a deck at all</h1>
    <p>
      Pioneer DJ's CDJs are the industry standard. Thousands of venues already have them set up, and
      DJs expect to find them when they walk into a booth: players that read the rekordbox stick
      they brought, with an interface they all know how to use.
    </p>
    <p>
      That standard has made Pioneer a monopoly in all but name, and a monopoly holds back what
      anyone else might invent. Its players are closed to the people who own them, too: there is no
      upgrading the hardware and no adding to what it can do. I believe that whoever buys and owns a
      piece of hardware should be free to do whatever they want with it, and never be locked out of
      it.
    </p>
    <p>
      I also wanted to learn, for myself, every skill it takes to put a finished product together:
      mechanical design and 3D printing, PCB design, embedded electronics and firmware, C++, audio
      processing, and the rest.
    </p>
    <p>
      A CDJ is not much more complex than a very large iPod. It plays music files out of a socket;
      the jog wheel, the buttons and the screen around it are interface. By building TriMixxx in the
      open, I want to give everyone curious about it a known base to build on and to iterate from,
      and to show the problems I ran into and how I solved them, so that building something like it
      yourself looks a lot less daunting.
    </p>
    <p class="signature">{site.author}</p>

    <h2>Built on</h2>
    <dl class="credits">
      {credits.map((credit) => (
        <>
          <dt>
            <a href={credit.href} rel="noopener">
              {credit.name}
            </a>
          </dt>
          <dd>{credit.what}</dd>
        </>
      ))}
    </dl>

    <h2>Source</h2>
    <p>
      Everything is on GitHub under free licences. The deck's own repository is GPL-3.0-or-later,
      hardware sources and documentation included; the projects it forks keep theirs.
    </p>
    <dl class="credits">
      {repositories.map((repo) => (
        <>
          <dt>
            <a href={repo.href} rel="noopener">
              {repo.name}
            </a>{" "}
            <span class="dim">{repo.licence}</span>
          </dt>
          <dd>{repo.what}</dd>
        </>
      ))}
    </dl>

    <h2>Get in touch</h2>
    <p>
      About the project, a collaboration, an event or the press:{" "}
      <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>. Bugs and code go to{" "}
      <a href={`${site.repo}/issues`} rel="noopener">
        GitHub issues
      </a>
      .
    </p>

    <h2>Fine print</h2>
    <p>
      TriMixxx is an independent project, with no affiliation with, sponsorship by or endorsement
      from AlphaTheta Corporation (formerly Pioneer DJ) or the Mixxx project.
    </p>
    <p>
      CDJ, rekordbox, Pro DJ Link, DJM, NXS and Pioneer DJ are trademarks of AlphaTheta Corporation.
      They appear here only to say which equipment TriMixxx works with, which protocol it speaks,
      and which decks it was measured against. Mixxx is the name of the independent open-source
      project at{" "}
      <a href={site.mixxx} rel="noopener">
        mixxx.org
      </a>
      ; TriMixxx is this project's own name, not a Mixxx product.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Pro DJ Link work is reverse engineering for interoperability, from network traffic between
      devices the author owns. Protocol facts are not copyrightable, and no code from any other
      implementation was copied. No Pioneer firmware is, or will be, in any TriMixxx repository.
    </p>
    <p>
      The build log shows an emulated CDJ-2000NXS at work. Its screen and lamps are drawn by
      AlphaTheta's own firmware, supplied by the player's owner; they appear only to document the
      emulator.
    </p>
    <p>
      <button type="button" class="settings" data-consent-open hidden>
        Analytics settings
      </button>
    </p>
  </article>
</Deck>

<style>
  .signature {
    color: var(--dim);
  }

  .signature::before {
    content: "— ";
  }

  .credits {
    margin: 0 0 1em;
  }

  .credits dt {
    margin-top: 0.9em;
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .credits dd {
    margin: 0.2em 0 0;
    color: var(--dim);
  }

  .settings {
    color: var(--dim);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 0.18em;
  }
</style>
